2. Step 1 — Install Java, Git, Maven, Docker and JMeter

Your terminal already has root privileges, so you can run these commands directly.
A. Update Ubuntu

Copy and execute:

apt update
apt upgrade -y

B. Install Java 21, Git, Maven and basic utilities

apt install -y openjdk-21-jdk git maven curl wget unzip ca-certificates

Java 21 is a good choice for developing modern Spring Boot applications.

Verify the installations:

java -version
javac -version
git --version
mvn -version

You should see the installed Java version, Git version and Maven version.
C. Install Docker and Docker Compose

Your MongoDB sharding and distributed storage assignments will benefit from Docker.

apt install -y docker.io docker-compose-v2

Start Docker and enable it to start automatically:

systemctl enable --now docker

Verify:

docker --version
docker compose version
docker info

Test Docker with a container:

docker run hello-world

If the test prints a success message, Docker is working.

Important: Your assignments use Docker Compose to run multiple MongoDB instances and Redis. You do not need to install MongoDB and Redis directly on Ubuntu if you run them in containers.
3. Step 2 — Install Visual Studio Code

You will use VS Code to develop all eight projects.

First, check whether it is already installed:

code --version

If you get a version number, you can skip the installation.

Otherwise, download the official VS Code Debian package from:

https://code.visualstudio.com/

Choose the .deb package for Debian/Ubuntu.

After downloading it, open your Downloads folder in the terminal and install it:

cd ~/Downloads
apt install ./code_*.deb

If the command cannot find the file, check the downloaded filename using:

ls -lh ~/Downloads

Then open VS Code:

code

Important: Your screenshot shows a root terminal. Although root can install system software, you should develop your projects using your regular Ubuntu account rather than running VS Code as root.
